{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.prompt.md; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.glm-5.3.findings.md; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.prompt.md; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.glm-5.3.findings.md; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-09-30 09:05:18 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-09-30 16:05:18 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6-sol, resumed) + agy (gemini-3.1-pro-high, resumed) now; glm (glm-5.3, resumed) after its usage cap resets\n     4\tSession-ID: codex 01a0f10a-6dcd-7af1-b670-78f74255321a | agy c1020f8c-3056-4129-a680-cdc1337b844a | glm 305a156d-0f83-4b26-8ed3-600af4c94594\n     5\t# Task: Architect mob — revision round: DHT on a raw datagram layer, dao.stream interfaces at both layers (OWNER DIRECTION)\n     6\tRole: Lead System Architect (mob participant)\n     7\tImplementers:\n     8\t- Model: gpt-6-sol | Assigned: 2026-09-30 16:05:18 +07 (+0700) | Status: active | Rationale: owner direction reverses mob D1/D2\n     9\t- Model: gemini-3.1-pro-high | Assigned: 2026-09-30 16:05:18 +07 (+0700) | Status: active | Rationale: same (design author)\n    10\t- Model: glm-5.3 | Assigned: (after GLM usage-cap reset) | Status: queued | Rationale: same\n    11\t\n    12\tRead-only; no edits; headless. Your prior consensus positions are in\n    13\tcollab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-consensus.{gemini-3.1-pro-high,gpt-6-sol,glm-5.3}.findings.md.\n    14\t\n    15\tOWNER DIRECTION (verbatim, in order):\n    16\t1. Owner question: \"can the udp transport have the same dao.stream surface API?\" — orchestrator answered: yes, today\n    17\t   dao.stream.udp's attach gives writer handles (append! sends) and readers read ring streams via projection/step!, and\n    18\t   dao.stream.remote builds full remote streams over it.\n    19\t2. Owner: \"does dao.stream.udp have its own interface other than the dao.stream interface?\"\n    20\t3. Owner: \"not all netwroking code is stream base but datagram base. stream base are built on top of datagrams. i want to\n    21\t   know if udp exposes datagram interface\" — orchestrator answered: at the VALUE level yes (send-to!/send-value! =\n    22\t   connectionless addressed sends of one canonical-CBOR value, fragmented over 1200 B, refused over max-message-bytes;\n    23\t   receive! deposits {:dao.stream.udp/attachment :dao.stream.udp/source :dao.stream.udp/value} events onto a traffic\n    24\t   medium), but there is NO portable RAW datagram layer: raw bytes exist only at the host send!/receive! seam.\n    25\t4. Owner DECISION: \"the dht should sit on the raw datagram layer but expose a dao.stream interface\"\n    26\t5. Owner CLARIFICATION (verbatim selected option): \"Both — A raw datagram layer exposed as dao.stream, AND a DHT whose\n    27\t   own API is dao.stream-shaped, built on it.\"\n    28\t\n    29\tThis supersedes consensus D1 (DHT over the dao.stream.udp VALUE/fragment layer) and reopens D2, D5, D6, D10. D3, D4, D7,\n    30\tD8, D9 stand unless this direction changes them (say so if it does).\n    31\t\n    32\tDesign, precisely enough to slice:\n    33\tR1. The raw datagram layer: namespace (split dao.stream.udp into a raw layer + the existing value/fragment message layer\n    34\t    rebuilt on top of it?), its dao.stream surface — a received-datagram stream ({bytes, source address} events? how are\n    35\t    bytes represented portably), sending by append! of {bytes, destination} values or per-destination writer handles,\n    36\t    descriptor shape, bounds (max datagram size, what append! answers for oversize: :dao.stream/invalid-value?), loss\n    37\t    semantics, cursor/gap/retention, host seams (JVM DatagramSocket, Node dgram, CLJD RawDatagramSocket), and how\n    38\t    dao.stream.udp's value layer and dao.stream.remote keep working unchanged on top of it.\n    39\tR2. The DHT on raw datagrams: its own wire (message framing per datagram, RPC correlation, codec — CBOR or other for\n    40\t    small Kademlia messages), how segment transfer larger than one datagram works (DHT-owned chunking vs reusing a\n    41\t    shared fragmenter over the raw layer — note the owner wants the DHT on the RAW layer, not the value layer), and\n    42\t    hardening (return-address cookies, rate limits, amplification bounds) at the raw layer.\n    43\tR3. The DHT's dao.stream-shaped public API: which streams (e.g. a request stream of put/get/find operations and a result\n    44\t    stream of outcomes; or content presented as stream handles), their value shapes and outcome vocabulary, correlation,\n    45\t    and how dao.jing's existing store-handle contract (materialize!/get/close!, used by yin.repl.index and\n    46\t    dao.jing.content) is satisfied — an adapter over the stream API? Keep the six invariants (no callbacks, explicit\n    47\t    state, caller-stepped).\n    48\tR4. Revised slice plan (S0...), concurrency, acceptance criteria, and dependencies (the durable-store epic for REPL\n    49\t    integration).\n    50\tR5. Items that must go to the OWNER.\n    51\t\n    52\tBegin the final response exactly with:\n    53\tCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\n    54\tCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n    55\tThen R1-R5 with file:line evidence; mark where you change your prior consensus position.\n     1\tCoding-Agent: agy\n     2\tSession-ID: c1020f8c-3056-4129-a680-cdc1337b844a\n     3\tModel: gemini-3.1-pro-high\n     4\t\n     5\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 09:06:07 GMT\n     6\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 16:06:07 +0700\n     7\t\n     8\t**R1. The raw datagram layer:**\n     9\t- **Namespace**: `dao.stream.datagram`. We split the raw socket host seams out of `dao.stream.udp` (`udp.cljc:264`, `udp.cljc:225`) and rebuild `dao.stream.udp`’s value/fragment layer on top of it. *(Changes consensus D1: isolates the raw layer)*.\n    10\t- **dao.stream surface**: A `(create-port)` returns a reader handle and a writer handle. The reader yields `{:dao.stream.datagram/bytes b, :dao.stream.datagram/source source-addr}` events. Bytes are portably represented as canonical byte arrays (`byte-array` / `Uint8Array` / `Uint8List`).\n    11\t- **Sending**: Appending to the writer handle expects `{:dao.stream.datagram/bytes b, :dao.stream.datagram/destination dest}`.\n    12\t- **Bounds**: `max-datagram` is strictly enforced (e.g., 1200 B). If a payload exceeds this, `append!` returns `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-value}`.\n    13\t- **Loss/Retention**: The reader is a zero-retention traffic stream (drops on lag); `append!` is best-effort and returns `:dao.stream/blocked` if the host's underlying socket buffer is full.\n    14\t- **Host Seams**: `DatagramSocket` (CLJ), `dgram` (CLJS), `RawDatagramSocket` (CLJD) are fully isolated inside this layer. `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.stream.remote` continue working seamlessly by taking `dao.stream.datagram` ports as dependencies.\n    15\t\n    16\t**R2. The DHT on raw datagrams:**\n    17\t- **Wire/Codec**: Canonical CBOR. Kademlia RPCs (`ping`, `find-node`) are mapped to small CBOR arrays/maps fitting in one 1200 B datagram. *(Changes consensus D2: confirms CBOR, but re-frames directly on raw datagrams)*.\n    18\t- **Chunking**: Because the DHT sits directly on the RAW layer, it CANNOT reuse `dao.stream.udp`'s fragment envelope (`udp.cljc:151`). The DHT MUST implement its own DRDS chunking logic for transferring segment payloads > 1200 B. *(Changes consensus D1/D5)*.\n    19\t- **Hardening**: Returnability cookies, token-bucket rate limits, and amplification bounds are implemented by the DHT at its raw datagram processing step, dropping abusive `{:dao.stream.datagram/source ...}` events before they trigger DHT logic or fragmentation reassembly.\n    20\t\n    21\t**R3. The DHT's dao.stream-shaped public API:**\n    22\t- **Streams**: The DHT exposes a `request-stream` (for operations) and a `result-stream` (for outcomes).\n    23\t- **Value Shapes**: `append!` to the request stream takes `{:dao.jing.dht/op :put, :dao.jing/address addr}` or `{:dao.jing.dht/op :get, :dao.jing/address addr}`. The result stream yields correlated `{:dao.jing/address addr, :dao.jing.dht/status :inserted}` or `{:dao.jing/address addr, :dao.jing/bytes bytes}` outcomes.\n    24\t- **Adapter Contract**: A `create-store-adapter` wraps these streams to satisfy `dao.jing`'s existing contract (`put-bytes-fn`, `get-bytes-fn`, `dht.cljc:255-278`). The adapter appends to the request stream and spin-polls/blocks on the result stream to return the synchronous values expected by `yin.repl.index` and `dao.jing.content`.\n    25\t- **Invariants**: The DHT has no hidden threads. It is explicit state advanced by a `(step! dht-state)` function pumped by the host event loop, fulfilling the caller-stepped invariant.\n    26\t\n    27\t**R4. Revised slice plan:**\n    28\t- **S0 (Datagram Layer)**: Extract host seams into `dao.stream.datagram` and refactor `dao.stream.udp` to sit on it. *AC*: existing `dao.stream.udp` tests pass unchanged.\n    29\t- **S1 (DHT Stream API & Local)**: Rewrite `dao.jing.dht` to expose the request/result stream API, implement the `dao.jing` store adapter, and define solo-net local mode.\n    30\t- **S2 (DHT Raw Wire & Chunking)**: Implement the CBOR wire protocol, DHT-owned chunking, and raw datagram port integration. *AC*: >1200B `put`/`get` succeeds across two raw peers.\n    31\t- **S3 (Hardening)**: Add returnability cookies and rate limits to the DHT raw datagram processing loop.\n    32\t- **S4 (yin.repl Integration)**: Wire `--dht-publish` flag and `:dht` spec (dependent on the durable-store epic).\n    33\t*Concurrency*: S0 blocks S2. S1 blocks S2. S0 and S1 can run concurrently. S2 blocks S3. S4 waits on durable-store.\n    34\t\n    35\t**R5. Items for the OWNER:**\n    36\t- By moving `get-bytes-fn` to an adapter over an async stream, the adapter must synchronously block (or spin-poll) to satisfy the legacy interface, unless `yin.repl` and `dao.jing.content` are also refactored to consume the async `result-stream` directly. Does the OWNER accept the synchronous adapter bridge, or require a cascading refactor of `dao.jing` to async streams?\n    37\t\n     1\tCoding-Agent: glm\n     2\tSession-ID: 305a156d-0f83-4b26-8ed3-600af4c94594\n     3\tModel: glm-5.3\n     4\t\n     5\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 09:10:00 GMT\n     6\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 16:10:00 +07 (+0700)\n     7\t\n     8\tRevision round under the owner direction (raw datagram layer exposed as dao.stream; DHT on the raw layer with a dao.stream-shaped API of its own). Positions weighed: gpt-6-sol revision, gemini-3.1-pro-high revision, my own consensus round. Ground truths I re-verified: the per-host socket seams already exist and are exactly the shape both designs need (`src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj` `bind!` → `{:socket :local-port :send! :close!}`; `src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs`; `src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd`); the writer outcome vocabulary is `ok/full/invalid-value/closed/transport-error`, with `blocked` a *reader* outcome (`docs/design/dao.stream.md:574,588-599`); and `dao.jing.content` already fixes the repo's bytes-on-streams convention as Base64 in the application value (`src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:11-13`).\n     9\t\n    10\t**Position changes, stated up front.** **D1: changed by owner direction, not by argument** — the DHT no longer rides `dao.stream.udp`'s value/fragment channel; it sits on a new raw layer and owns its framing. My consensus recommendation is superseded; what survives from it is the *extraction discipline* (chunk/reassembly rules as a shared transport-neutral utility under DHT-owned fields, not a private copy) and the requirement that the value layer and `dao.stream.remote` keep working unchanged on top. **D2: conclusion unchanged (CBOR), reason changed** — my consensus reason (forced by the §3.2 channel rule) is gone with D1; re-derived on raw-layer grounds below, with new evidence that decides it. **D5: mechanism changed, spirit unchanged** — non-JVM hosts arrive via the raw layer's existing host seams; the \"hardening written once\" property survives because hardening lives in the host-neutral DHT interpreter above the raw port. **D6: unchanged** (design frozen at S0, implementation before non-loopback exposure). **D3, D4, D7, D8, D9 stand** — and D3 is *better expressed* by this architecture: the put outcome is the local verdict, replication progress is a separate event, so the honest-ack contract becomes structural rather than documentary. D4's solo mode is now simply \"no raw port composed\". D8's sync-lookup fix becomes structural too (the interpreter resolves peers in its own step, never on the caller's).\n    11\t\n    12\t## R1. The raw datagram layer\n    13\t\n    14\t**Namespace: `dao.stream.datagram`** (agree with gemini over gpt's `dao.stream.udp.raw`): the owner framed the concept as \"not all networking code is stream base but datagram base\" — the abstraction is *datagram*, and UDP is its one carrier today; a sub-namespace of `udp` would invert that (the value/fragment layer is the udp-specific part, `dao.stream.remote.md` §3.2, and stays `dao.stream.udp`). Either name works; the load-bearing point is that it is a sibling transport with its own key namespace, not a rewrite of the value layer.\n    15\t\n    16\t**Surface.** A bound port is created (`create!`-style, like the memory-log — there is no remote descriptor to attach to) and exposes exactly: one **writer handle** whose `append!` value is `{:dao.stream.datagram/destination {:host h :port p} :dao.stream.datagram/bytes b64}`, and a **traffic deposit** of `{:dao.stream.datagram/source {:host h :port p} :dao.stream.datagram/bytes b64}` events onto a caller-supplied stream. The socket itself has no reader surface (it retains nothing — the existing deposit model, `udp.cljc:364-370`); consumers hold cursors on the traffic stream; a bounded ring's `gap` is datagram loss by definition and each interpreter treats it as such. Sending is destination-in-value (connectionless); per-destination writer handles are sugar composable above it (the `UdpHandle` shape, `udp.cljc:460-476`), not part of the layer.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Bytes as Base64 text in the public values** (agree with gpt, differ from gemini's \"canonical byte arrays\"). Three grounds: the host-boundary rule — \"Host types are classified into plain data in the transform… no host type crosses onto the stream\" (`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries) — and `byte[]`/`Uint8Array`/`Uint8List` are host types; the repo's settled convention for bytes in stream-visible application values is padded standard Base64 (`content.cljc:11-13`; `dao.jing.cbor.md:464-471`); and the Transit codec carries no byte strings (`dao.jing.md`, open items), so gemini's choice would fence a datagram traffic stream off from ever being served through half the remote surface. Real bytes stay below the boundary at the host seam, exactly where they live today (`send!` takes bytes, `receive!` takes bytes, `udp.cljc:202,373`). The encode/decode cost per datagram is trivial beside the syscalls.\n    19\t\n    20\t**Outcomes and bounds.** `append!` answers `:dao.stream/ok` (local send acceptance — never delivery, per `dao.stream.md:588-599` Writing and `dao.stream.remote.md` §2.4's identical rule), `:dao.stream/invalid-value` (malformed address/Base64, or decoded length over the enforced 1200 B `max-datagram`, `udp.cljc:58-62`), `:dao.stream/closed`, `:dao.stream/transport-error` (host send fault). Here I **differ from gemini**: their \"`append!` returns `:dao.stream/blocked` if the host's socket buffer is full\" is a contract error — `blocked` is a reader outcome (`dao.stream.md:574`); a writer under back pressure answers `full` (`dao.stream.md:161-162,595`), and only if a bounded outbound queue is composed (host policy), otherwise `transport-error`. gpt's outcome list is correct as written.\n    21\t\n    22\t**Migration.** The host seams move under the raw layer unchanged in shape (`jvm.clj`/`node.cljs`/`dart.cljd` already return exactly this `send!`/deposit seam); `dao.stream.udp`'s value layer becomes an interpreter *over* the raw port: `make-port`'s `:send!` host seam and the direct `receive!` call become composition internals, the fragment/reassembly machinery (`udp.cljc:151-195,316-361`), descriptor, projection, `step!`, and `send-to!` are unchanged publicly, and `dao.stream.remote`'s §3.2 channel is untouched. Acceptance is both positions' criterion combined: existing `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.stream.remote` tests pass unchanged (gemini's S0 AC), plus new per-host and cross-host raw round-trip tests of exact bytes and observed source addresses (gpt's S1 AC). Host callbacks only deposit and return (`datom.world.md:69`), as today.\n    23\t\n    24\t## R2. The DHT on raw datagrams\n    25\t\n    26\t**Codec: canonical CBOR now, with a `:ver` field on every message.** Here I **differ from gpt** (keep Transit, version later) and **agree with gemini's conclusion while adding the evidence neither gave**: with k=20, a `find-node` reply carrying 20 `{id host port}` peer maps is ~2 KB in Transit-JSON — *already over the 1200 B budget today*, and therefore silently dropped at `node.cljc:190-195` whenever a table is deep enough to answer with a full bucket. The datagram budget is not a cosmetic constraint on this wire; it is binding on the most common message. CBOR byte-string ids bring a peer record to ~60–70 B, and a **capped peers-per-reply** (nearest 8, with shortlist iteration compensating — `dht.cljc:107-131` already iterates) fits comfortably. Add to that codec-family uniformity (content is canonical CBOR; `dao.jing.cbor.md:469` describes the Transit envelope as current state, not a commitment) and one codec story for the non-JVM hosts, and CBOR wins on raw-layer grounds my consensus position never needed. I adopt gpt's version-field discipline, which gemini lacks: `:ver 1` on every message now, so the chunk messages of the next revision need no fleet flag day. No negotiation machinery; the clean break is free while there is no fleet.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Chunking: DHT-owned, rules borrowed, fields its own.** Forced by the owner's decision (the DHT may not consume the value layer's envelope). Each DHT message is one CBOR value; ≤1200 B encoded → one datagram; larger → `{ver, rpc, dir, part, parts, bytes}` datagrams whose `bytes` are slices of the whole encoded message, reassembled keyed by `[observed source address, dir, rpc]` — the same key shape the value layer uses (`[attachment direction id]`, `udp.cljc:328-332`) — then decoded whole, then hash-verified by the existing ingress check (`dht.cljc:167-180`), never stored or served otherwise. The reassembly *rules* are re-implemented against DHT fields but should come from one shared transport-neutral utility parameterized over key and fields (gpt explicitly allows this and bars importing `udp.cljc`'s private envelope or its `:dao.stream.remote/id` meaning — I agree; this is the surviving fragment of my consensus D1). Bounds: `max-message-bytes` (64 KiB default, composition datum), parts-count disagreement drops, duplicates ignored, accumulated-bytes bound enforced on arrival, oldest-partial eviction, per-source *and* global partials caps — the value layer's discipline (`udp.cljc:310-361`) minus its stream namespaces. Oversize is refused before the first datagram, never torn (`udp.cljc:225-233` rule).\n    29\t\n    30\t**Hardening at this boundary.** Both positions put it in the DHT's raw processing step; agree, and the consensus D6 gate survives unchanged: the cookie/caps *design* is frozen in S0, implemented in the hardening slice, and nothing runs non-loopback before it. Adopt gpt's additions, which improve on both earlier rounds: a small `ping` obtains a time-limited cookie bound to the **observed source ip:port** (not the claimed `:from`); the cookie is required before any fragmented reply and before allocating reassembly state, and a verifiable token rides each incoming chunk so an attacker cannot force assembly before validation; unverified replies are capped at the request's size or a small fixed budget; replies are matched to the expected observed source, not the rpc number alone (`node.cljc:176-178` currently matches by `:rpc` only); and — the sharpest catch — **do not observe the claimed `:from` into the routing table before validation** (`node.cljc:171-175` does exactly that today). Amplification framing from my consensus round stands: chunking turns ~12× into ~60×, which is why the design gate belongs to S0. Rate limiting stays LRU-capped per source (bounded memory against port rotation), with per-source and global caps on work, fragments, pending RPCs, and storage.\n    31\t\n    32\t## R3. The DHT's dao.stream-shaped API\n    33\t\n    34\t**Two composition-supplied streams, caller-stepped interpreter — agree with both positions' shape, adopt gpt's value details.** A request writer and an outcome stream; request values carry a caller-minted id: `{:dao.jing.dht/id n :dao.jing.dht/op :put :dao.jing.dht/address a :dao.jing.dht/bytes b64}`, `{:dao.jing.dht/id n :dao.jing.dht/op :get :dao.jing.dht/address a}`, plus `:find` for shortlist queries. The interpreter is a `(step! dht)` that reads requests and raw traffic, owns explicit pending-RPC and cursor state, and appends exactly one terminal outcome per accepted id: put → the **local verdict** `{:id n :dao.jing.dht/put :inserted|:present}` emitted as soon as the local backend answers (D3 made structural), get → `{:id n :dao.jing.dht/get :found b64 | :not-found | :error …}`; replication progress is a separate informational event keyed by address and can never retroactively change a verdict. These are DHT-qualified *values* on the outcome stream, while stream operations keep the exhaustive `:dao.stream/outcome` vocabulary (`dao.stream.md:94-96` Result Convention) — gpt states this distinction exactly right. **Differ from gemini** on two specifics: their outcome values carry no correlation id (an address alone cannot correlate concurrent or repeated operations), and `spin-polls/blocks` is not an acceptable adapter mechanism — the repo's established pattern is a portable stepped core plus a **JVM blocking facade** that sleeps between steps with explicit deadlines (`dao.jing.md:436-441` records the precedent for the deleted `dao.jing.remote`; `dao.jing.content.driver` is the current instance, `content.cljc:16`), never a spin loop.\n    35\t\n    36\t**The dao.jing adapter** (the crux, and where the two positions genuinely fork). Keep the byte-store handle contract (`:put-bytes-fn/:get-bytes-fn/:close-fn`, consumed by `dao.jing/materialize!`, `dao.jing.content`, and `yin.repl.index` at `index.cljc:148-160`) satisfied by an adapter with **three postures**: (1) `put` is always synchronous-and-local — validate (`dht.cljc:155-164`), write `:local`, enqueue the address-only volatile intent, return the verdict; this is honest on every host with no waiting at all; (2) `get` on a local hit answers immediately; (3) a local miss is *staged* on non-JVM hosts — the adapter issues the request, answers the caller per an explicit policy (an outstanding-fetch marker, never a silent `not-found`, gpt's rule), and hydration completes on later steps with the caller retrying the local read. On the JVM only, a blocking facade may drive `step!` to a deadline and return the verified fetch synchronously — preserving today's `make-get` semantics (`dht.cljc:227-252`) for callers that want them. This differs from my consensus round only in *where* the sync surface lives: previously the handle itself did network IO; now the core never blocks and blocking is a facade, which is the better factoring and the one the repo already uses everywhere else. Note what this does for the REPL round: `yin.repl.index` reads back only what it just wrote, so its publish/readback path (`index.cljc:232-252`) stays entirely on local hits — the round never waits on the network even without any facade.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Unchanged standings:** solo mode is \"no raw port composed\" (D4); `mem` default, `dht:` explicit, the structural `--dht-publish` gate (D9) — with the gate now enforced at **both** ends gpt names: origin (the store refuses to enqueue replication intent for non-public content while closed) and serving (bootstrap-alone is fetch/serve of already-public content, never originating publication). D7 unchanged: local HEAD covers restart; a reader given only a peer address still needs a supplied manifest address until the root-facts epic exists.\n    39\t\n    40\t## R4. Revised slice plan\n    41\t\n    42\tMatching gpt's skeleton (which is the better of the two — gemini's bundles the stream-API rewrite, adapter, and solo mode into one slice and has no contract-freeze step), with my amendments:\n    43\t\n    44\t- **S0 — Contract freeze (docs, no behavior).** Raw value shapes/outcomes, DHT request/outcome values, CBOR wire + `:ver`, chunk fields and all bounds, peers-per-reply cap, cookie/caps design (D6 gate), facade vs staged-hydration boundary; fix the stale `dao.jing.dht.md:19` line and the `dao.jing.cbor.md:469` DHT-envelope sentence; add the raw layer to the design docs. *AC:* every `append!` outcome and every asynchronous completion enumerated; no sentence equates send acceptance with delivery.\n    45\t- **S1 — Raw datagram layer (`dao.stream.datagram`).** Host seams move under it; value channel rebuilt on top; public udp/remote surfaces unchanged. *AC:* per-host and cross-host raw round-trips of exact bytes + observed source; oversize → `invalid-value`; ring gap observable as loss; **all existing `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.stream.remote` tests pass unchanged**.\n    46\t- **S2 — DHT stream core + solo + adapter (parallel with S1 after S0).** Request/outcome streams, caller-stepped interpreter, local verdict semantics, address-only volatile intent, JVM facade, solo net. *AC:* put does no lookup and no peer wait; ids correlate under concurrent requests; solo composes no raw port and no intent queue; file-backed acks survive restart; facade deadlines honored; no threads/callbacks in the core.\n    47\t- **S3 — DHT raw wire + bounded chunk transfer (after S1+S2).** CBOR + `:ver`, rpc correlation, capped peers-per-reply, chunk protocol with shared-utility reassembly, whole-content verification. *AC:* 2–3 node JVM and cross-host transfers of >1200 B and ~64 KiB segments succeed under injected deterministic loss/reordering; oversize and malformed chunks stay within every declared bound; loopback-only exposure.\n    48\t- **S4 — Hardening + lookup repair + storage bound (after S3).** Cookies, source matching, per-source/global caps, `:from`-after-validation, dead-peer shortlist replacement, local storage bound with loud refusal. *AC:* a spoofed source cannot obtain a fragmented reply or force reassembly; over-limit peers cost bounded memory; a live next-nearest peer serves when nearer peers are dead.\n    49\t- **S5 — REPL integration (externally gated on the durable-store epic).** `dht:<dir>` spec, solo default, dir lock (two processes ⇒ two dirs + loopback bootstrap), HEAD unchanged, bootstrap ⇒ fetch/serve, `--dht-publish` gate, remote query from a supplied manifest address with staged hydration. *AC:* local-only restart sequence per the durable findings §5; B's `q` resolves A's manifest over the wire; default start opens no socket.\n    50\t\n    51\t**Concurrency, honestly:** S0 → {S1 ∥ S2} → S3 → S4 → S5, with S1∥S2 legitimate because file ownership is disjoint (raw layer + udp refactor vs `dao/jing/dht*` core) and S0 froze the interfaces both consume. No slice before S5 touches `yin/repl/*`. This matches gpt; gemini's S0∥S1→S2→S3→S4 is the same graph under different names, minus the freeze slice.\n    52\t\n    53\t## R5. Items for the OWNER\n    54\t\n    55\t1. **Publication policy, both ends** (adopt gpt's framing, which sharpens my D9.3): ratify that explicit public opt-in (`--dht-publish`) may publish REPL code before ShiBi, or prohibit all REPL-code publication until ShiBi — and note the gate is enforceable at origin *and* serving, so the answer should say whether serving cached third-party content under bootstrap-alone is acceptable in the interim.\n    56\t2. **Remote-read scope** (gpt's #2, = my standing D7): with local HEAD covering restart, a reader holding only a peer address cannot find the *latest* manifest. Supplied-manifest-address is this epic's scope; automatic latest-root discovery is a separate root-facts epic (signed chain, blocked on naming + ShiBi). The owner should confirm they accept \"supplied address now, root facts later\".\n    57\t3. **The synchronous boundary** (gpt's #3 and gemini's R5 are the same question; my recommendation): ratify the repo pattern — portable non-blocking stream core + JVM-only blocking facade + staged hydration elsewhere — rather than a cascading async refactor of `dao.jing`'s byte-store handle contract, whose blast radius includes `yin.repl.index` and `dao.jing.content`. The cascading refactor is a defensible future epic, not a rider on this one.\n    58\t4. *(Architect-decidable, flagged for transparency, not owner)*: peers-per-reply capped to what fits one 1200 B datagram (nearest ~8, iteration compensates) — it mildly shapes DHT convergence vs traffic, so the owner may care; and the raw layer's name (`dao.stream.datagram`).\n    59\t\n    60\t## Where I agree and differ\n    61\t\n    62\t**With gpt-6-sol:** agree on R1 almost wholly (Base64, outcome vocabulary, destination-in-value, no reader surface on the socket, migration with tests-unchanged AC), on R3's architecture and value shapes, on R4's skeleton and R5's items — this position and mine converge on nearly everything load-bearing. **Differ on the codec** (keep Transit vs my CBOR-now): the deciding evidence is that a k=20 `find-node` reply is ~2 KB in Transit and is *already being silently dropped* at `node.cljc:190-195` on full buckets — the budget binds the protocol's most common message, and CBOR plus a capped peers-per-reply is the only version of the common path that fits. I also add the peers-per-reply cap and the `:ver`-field adoption as explicit wire constraints gpt's position left implicit.\n    63\t\n    64\t**With gemini-3.1-pro-high:** agree on the namespace (`dao.stream.datagram`), CBOR, the `step!`-driven caller-stepped interpreter, and the slice graph's shape. **Differ on four specifics:** host byte arrays in stream values (violates the host-boundary rule and the `content.cljc:11-13` Base64 convention, and fences the traffic stream off Transit-carrying channels); `append!` answering `:dao.stream/blocked` (a reader outcome, `dao.stream.md:574`; writers answer `full`, `:595`, and only over a composed bounded queue); outcome values without correlation ids; and the spin-polling adapter (blocking facade with sleep-and-deadline is the established pattern, `dao.jing.md:436-441`). Its slice plan also lacks a contract-freeze step and over-bundles S1.\n    65\t\n    66\t**Changes to my prior consensus positions, restated in one line each:** D1 superseded by owner direction (extraction discipline survives inside R2's chunk utility); D2 conclusion kept, reason replaced (reply-size math, not the §3.2 channel rule) plus gpt's version field; D5 mechanism swapped to raw-layer host seams with hardening still written once; D6 unchanged; D3, D4, D7, D8, D9 stand, with D3 and D8's fixes now structural in the stream-shaped API.\n    67\t\n    68\tA note on process: read-only as instructed; nothing in the repository was edited.\n     1\tCoding-Agent: codex\n     2\tSession-ID: 01a0f10a-6dcd-7af1-b670-78f74255321a\n     3\tModel: gpt-6-sol (pinned)\n     4\t\n     5\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 09:07:00 GMT\n     6\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 16:07:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     7\t\n     8\t## R1. Raw datagram layer\n     9\t\n    10\t**I change my prior D1 position.** The owner has chosen a raw datagram boundary, so the DHT must not consume `dao.stream.udp`’s decoded, fragmented value channel.\n    11\t\n    12\tAdd `dao.stream.udp.raw` beneath the existing `dao.stream.udp`. A bound raw port exposes a `dao.stream` **writer handle**. Its `append!` value is plain data:\n    13\t\n    14\t```clojure\n    15\t{:dao.stream.udp.raw/to    {:host \"198.51.100.7\" :port 4040}\n    16\t :dao.stream.udp.raw/bytes \"<padded standard Base64>\"}\n    17\t```\n    18\t\n    19\tBase64 represents the exact datagram bytes across CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD without placing host byte-array types in stream values. The raw layer decodes it, checks destination and a **1200-byte decoded datagram limit**, and makes one addressed send. Malformed address, Base64, or oversize yields `:dao.stream/invalid-value`; a closed port yields `:dao.stream/closed`; socket failure yields `:dao.stream/transport-error`. `:dao.stream/ok` means local send acceptance, never delivery, as the stream writing contract requires ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:588)). No raw-layer retry, ordering, reassembly, or content decoding occurs.\n    20\t\n    21\tThe socket adapter deposits each received datagram onto a caller-supplied traffic stream as `{:dao.stream.udp.raw/source {:host h :port p}, :dao.stream.udp.raw/bytes b64}`. It copies bytes before deposit and takes the source from the socket, not the payload. The socket itself has **no reader surface** because it retains nothing; consumers hold cursors on the supplied traffic stream. A bounded ring may report `:dao.stream/gap`, which each DHT or value-channel interpreter must treat as lost datagrams. This follows the explicit boundary and retention rules ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:405), [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:570)). The raw writer’s descriptor names that bound port and its identity; its destination remains in each append value. Binding is a host-composed creation operation, and closing closes the port. Do not imply that attaching to a remote port grants a remote reader surface.\n    22\t\n    23\tRefactor the existing `dao.stream.udp` value channel to read raw traffic with an owned cursor and send encoded fragment datagrams through the raw writer. Its public value-channel descriptor, `send-to!`, projection, and `dao.stream.remote` behavior stay the same. Today `dao.stream.udp/receive!` accepts raw bytes directly and `make-port` takes a host `:send!` function ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:202), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:373)); those become composition internals. Existing JVM, Node, and Dart socket seams already receive source addresses and raw bytes ([jvm.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj:12), [node.cljs](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs:14), [dart.cljd](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd:13)). Their host callbacks may only deposit an event and return, per the host-boundary invariant ([datom.world.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:69)).\n    24\t\n    25\t## R2. DHT directly on raw datagrams\n    26\t\n    27\t**I also change my prior D2 position:** canonical CBOR is no longer forced by the value channel. Keep the present Transit-JSON encoding for small DHT RPC maps in the first migration, and version the DHT wire before adding chunk messages. The node currently sends one Transit-JSON map per datagram ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:1), [node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:57)). A later CBOR change needs its own interop reason and versioned fleet plan; content payloads remain canonical CBOR regardless of the DHT envelope ([dao.jing.cbor.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:462)).\n    28\t\n    29\tThe DHT interpreter reads raw traffic, decodes only DHT wire values, and emits replies by appending addressed raw datagrams. Small `ping`, `find-node`, `store-content`, `fetch-content`, and reply messages retain RPC IDs. For messages exceeding one datagram, define a **DHT-owned chunk protocol** over raw datagrams: version, sender request ID, request/reply direction, part index, total parts, and chunk bytes. Correlate partials by observed source address and port plus those fields. A shared, transport-neutral fragmentation *utility* is acceptable, but it must not import `dao.stream.udp`’s private envelope or its `dao.stream.remote/id` meaning ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151)). Bound encoded message size, number and bytes of partials per source and globally, and number of parts. Refuse oversize before the first send; duplicate or inconsistent parts do not grow state. Evict incomplete partials under a declared policy; retry the complete RPC after timeout. Verify the complete content against its segment address before storing or caching, as the current DHT does ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:167)).\n    30\t\n    31\tHardening belongs to the DHT **at this raw boundary**. A small `ping` can obtain a time-limited cookie bound to the observed source IP and port. Require it before a fragmented reply or allocating substantial incoming reassembly state; put a verifiable token on each incoming chunk so an attacker cannot force assembly before validation. Cap unverified replies to the request size or a small fixed budget. Match RPC replies to the expected observed source, limit work and storage per source and globally, and do not add a claimed `:from` to routing before validation. The current receiver observes `:from` before processing and matches replies only by RPC ID ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:171)). These rules must be specified with chunking; non-loopback exposure waits for their implementation.\n    32\t\n    33\t## R3. DHT’s stream-shaped API and Jing adapter\n    34\t\n    35\tExpose two composition-supplied streams: a **request writer** and an **outcome reader**. Request values carry a caller-minted correlation ID and one operation:\n    36\t\n    37\t```clojure\n    38\t{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :put\n    39\t :dao.jing.dht/address address :dao.jing.dht/bytes b64}\n    40\t{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :get\n    41\t :dao.jing.dht/address address}\n    42\t{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :find\n    43\t :dao.jing.dht/target target-id}\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\tThe caller’s `append!` outcome says only that the request stream accepted the value. A caller-stepped DHT interpreter reads requests and raw traffic, owns explicit pending-RPC and cursor state, and appends exactly one terminal outcome per accepted request ID: `:stored-locally` with `:inserted|:present`, `:found` with verified bytes, `:not-found`, `:refused`, `:timeout`, or `:error`. Replication progress is a separate event stream keyed by address; it cannot retroactively change the local put verdict. Results use DHT-qualified *values*, while stream operations continue to use the exhaustive `:dao.stream/outcome` vocabulary ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:96)). Duplicate request IDs and completion retention need explicit bounded policy; no callbacks or hidden worker are required.\n    47\t\n    48\tKeep the existing synchronous Jing byte-store handle as a **local compatibility adapter**. Its `put` validates and writes to the local backend, then emits an address-only replication intent; `:inserted`/`:present` means the local backend verdict. Its `get` answers local verified content immediately. A remote miss is requested and hydrated through the DHT streams, then the caller retries the local read. A synchronous, portable `get` cannot wait for a future network answer: DaoStream explicitly says operations do not wait ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:69)). The current DHT handle *does* synchronously fetch on a miss ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:227)); that behavior must be isolated as a JVM blocking facade, or callers must migrate to staged hydration. Do not silently return `not-found` for an outstanding remote fetch. `yin.repl.index` can continue its synchronous local write and manifest readback ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:232)); remote query needs a hydration step before `q`. The repository already distinguishes a stepped content client from its JVM blocking driver ([content.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:18)).\n    49\t\n    50\tThe prior D3, D4, D7, D8, and D9 decisions stand: volatile best-effort replication intent with an honest local acknowledgement; a solo net with no socket; latest-root discovery outside this DHT epic, with local HEAD for owner restart; lookup and resource-bound repairs here; `mem` default and explicit network/publication choices. The raw-layer decision changes none of those contracts.\n    51\t\n    52\t## R4. Revised slices\n    53\t\n    54\t| Slice | Work | Acceptance |\n    55\t|---|---|---|\n    56\t| **S0: Contract freeze** | Specify raw values/descriptors, DHT request/result values, wire version, chunk and abuse budgets, local-versus-remote Jing behavior. | The design states every `append!` outcome and every asynchronous completion; no claim equates send acceptance with delivery. |\n    57\t| **S1: Raw datagram stream** | Build `dao.stream.udp.raw` over the existing host seams; make the value channel an interpreter above it. | JVM, Node, and Dart raw streams round-trip exact binary datagrams and observed source addresses; oversize is `invalid-value`; loss and ring gaps are observable; existing value-channel and `dao.stream.remote` tests still pass. |\n    58\t| **S2: DHT stream core and solo mode** | Request/outcome streams, caller-stepped interpreter, local Jing adapter, volatile address-only replication intent. | Put has no lookup or peer wait; outcomes correlate under concurrent requests; solo mode opens no socket or replication queue; local file-backed acknowledgements survive restart. |\n    59\t| **S3: DHT raw wire and bounded chunk transfer** | Connect DHT interpreter directly to raw traffic/writer; add DHT-owned chunking and whole-content verification. | Multi-node transfer of index-sized segments succeeds with reordering/loss and explicit retries; oversize and malformed chunks stay within all declared bounds. Only loopback exposure is allowed at this stage. |\n    60\t| **S4: Hardening and lookup repair** | Cookies, source matching, rate/resource limits, dead-peer replacement. | Spoofed requests cannot trigger fragmented amplification or substantial reassembly; a live next-nearest peer remains fetchable when closer peers fail. |\n    61\t| **S5: REPL integration** | After the durable-store epic supplies store-spec, HEAD, locking, and recovery, add explicit DHT choice, public-data gate, and remote hydration/query entry point requiring a supplied manifest address. | Local-only restart works without a socket; separate processes use separate locked directories; remote query works from a supplied root; source/index content is neither published nor served without explicit public opt-in. |\n    62\t\n    63\tS1 can run alongside S2 **only after S0**, with separate file and test ownership; S3 depends on both. S4’s design is part of S0, and its implementation follows S3 before exposure. S5 is externally dependent on the durable-store epic. No earlier slice touches `yin/repl/*`.\n    64\t\n    65\t## R5. Items for the owner\n    66\t\n    67\t1. **Confirm the publication policy:** whether explicit public-index opt-in may publish REPL code before ShiBi, or whether all REPL code publication is prohibited until ShiBi. Either choice must be enforced at both origin and serving paths.\n    68\t2. **Confirm the remote-read scope:** local HEAD covers restart, while a reader given only a peer address still cannot discover the latest manifest. Automatic latest-root discovery would require a separate root-fact design or an added epic slice.\n    69\t3. **Choose the synchronous compatibility boundary:** permit a JVM-only blocking DHT `get` facade, or require all remote reads to use staged hydration. The stream-shaped API itself remains nonblocking on every host.\n    70\t\n    71\tThe owner’s raw-layer decision settles the previous D1/D2 transport dependency: the DHT owns its datagram protocol, while both the raw port and the DHT expose `dao.stream` interfaces.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.glm-5.3.findings.md | sed -n '24,60p'; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-consensus.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md | sed -n '8,64p'; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-consensus.glm-5.3.findings.md | sed -n '8,60p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.glm-5.3.findings.md | sed -n '24,60p'; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-consensus.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md | sed -n '8,64p'; nl -ba collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-consensus.glm-5.3.findings.md | sed -n '8,60p'\"","aggregated_output":"    24\t## R2. The DHT on raw datagrams\n    25\t\n    26\t**Codec: canonical CBOR now, with a `:ver` field on every message.** Here I **differ from gpt** (keep Transit, version later) and **agree with gemini's conclusion while adding the evidence neither gave**: with k=20, a `find-node` reply carrying 20 `{id host port}` peer maps is ~2 KB in Transit-JSON — *already over the 1200 B budget today*, and therefore silently dropped at `node.cljc:190-195` whenever a table is deep enough to answer with a full bucket. The datagram budget is not a cosmetic constraint on this wire; it is binding on the most common message. CBOR byte-string ids bring a peer record to ~60–70 B, and a **capped peers-per-reply** (nearest 8, with shortlist iteration compensating — `dht.cljc:107-131` already iterates) fits comfortably. Add to that codec-family uniformity (content is canonical CBOR; `dao.jing.cbor.md:469` describes the Transit envelope as current state, not a commitment) and one codec story for the non-JVM hosts, and CBOR wins on raw-layer grounds my consensus position never needed. I adopt gpt's version-field discipline, which gemini lacks: `:ver 1` on every message now, so the chunk messages of the next revision need no fleet flag day. No negotiation machinery; the clean break is free while there is no fleet.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Chunking: DHT-owned, rules borrowed, fields its own.** Forced by the owner's decision (the DHT may not consume the value layer's envelope). Each DHT message is one CBOR value; ≤1200 B encoded → one datagram; larger → `{ver, rpc, dir, part, parts, bytes}` datagrams whose `bytes` are slices of the whole encoded message, reassembled keyed by `[observed source address, dir, rpc]` — the same key shape the value layer uses (`[attachment direction id]`, `udp.cljc:328-332`) — then decoded whole, then hash-verified by the existing ingress check (`dht.cljc:167-180`), never stored or served otherwise. The reassembly *rules* are re-implemented against DHT fields but should come from one shared transport-neutral utility parameterized over key and fields (gpt explicitly allows this and bars importing `udp.cljc`'s private envelope or its `:dao.stream.remote/id` meaning — I agree; this is the surviving fragment of my consensus D1). Bounds: `max-message-bytes` (64 KiB default, composition datum), parts-count disagreement drops, duplicates ignored, accumulated-bytes bound enforced on arrival, oldest-partial eviction, per-source *and* global partials caps — the value layer's discipline (`udp.cljc:310-361`) minus its stream namespaces. Oversize is refused before the first datagram, never torn (`udp.cljc:225-233` rule).\n    29\t\n    30\t**Hardening at this boundary.** Both positions put it in the DHT's raw processing step; agree, and the consensus D6 gate survives unchanged: the cookie/caps *design* is frozen in S0, implemented in the hardening slice, and nothing runs non-loopback before it. Adopt gpt's additions, which improve on both earlier rounds: a small `ping` obtains a time-limited cookie bound to the **observed source ip:port** (not the claimed `:from`); the cookie is required before any fragmented reply and before allocating reassembly state, and a verifiable token rides each incoming chunk so an attacker cannot force assembly before validation; unverified replies are capped at the request's size or a small fixed budget; replies are matched to the expected observed source, not the rpc number alone (`node.cljc:176-178` currently matches by `:rpc` only); and — the sharpest catch — **do not observe the claimed `:from` into the routing table before validation** (`node.cljc:171-175` does exactly that today). Amplification framing from my consensus round stands: chunking turns ~12× into ~60×, which is why the design gate belongs to S0. Rate limiting stays LRU-capped per source (bounded memory against port rotation), with per-source and global caps on work, fragments, pending RPCs, and storage.\n    31\t\n    32\t## R3. The DHT's dao.stream-shaped API\n    33\t\n    34\t**Two composition-supplied streams, caller-stepped interpreter — agree with both positions' shape, adopt gpt's value details.** A request writer and an outcome stream; request values carry a caller-minted id: `{:dao.jing.dht/id n :dao.jing.dht/op :put :dao.jing.dht/address a :dao.jing.dht/bytes b64}`, `{:dao.jing.dht/id n :dao.jing.dht/op :get :dao.jing.dht/address a}`, plus `:find` for shortlist queries. The interpreter is a `(step! dht)` that reads requests and raw traffic, owns explicit pending-RPC and cursor state, and appends exactly one terminal outcome per accepted id: put → the **local verdict** `{:id n :dao.jing.dht/put :inserted|:present}` emitted as soon as the local backend answers (D3 made structural), get → `{:id n :dao.jing.dht/get :found b64 | :not-found | :error …}`; replication progress is a separate informational event keyed by address and can never retroactively change a verdict. These are DHT-qualified *values* on the outcome stream, while stream operations keep the exhaustive `:dao.stream/outcome` vocabulary (`dao.stream.md:94-96` Result Convention) — gpt states this distinction exactly right. **Differ from gemini** on two specifics: their outcome values carry no correlation id (an address alone cannot correlate concurrent or repeated operations), and `spin-polls/blocks` is not an acceptable adapter mechanism — the repo's established pattern is a portable stepped core plus a **JVM blocking facade** that sleeps between steps with explicit deadlines (`dao.jing.md:436-441` records the precedent for the deleted `dao.jing.remote`; `dao.jing.content.driver` is the current instance, `content.cljc:16`), never a spin loop.\n    35\t\n    36\t**The dao.jing adapter** (the crux, and where the two positions genuinely fork). Keep the byte-store handle contract (`:put-bytes-fn/:get-bytes-fn/:close-fn`, consumed by `dao.jing/materialize!`, `dao.jing.content`, and `yin.repl.index` at `index.cljc:148-160`) satisfied by an adapter with **three postures**: (1) `put` is always synchronous-and-local — validate (`dht.cljc:155-164`), write `:local`, enqueue the address-only volatile intent, return the verdict; this is honest on every host with no waiting at all; (2) `get` on a local hit answers immediately; (3) a local miss is *staged* on non-JVM hosts — the adapter issues the request, answers the caller per an explicit policy (an outstanding-fetch marker, never a silent `not-found`, gpt's rule), and hydration completes on later steps with the caller retrying the local read. On the JVM only, a blocking facade may drive `step!` to a deadline and return the verified fetch synchronously — preserving today's `make-get` semantics (`dht.cljc:227-252`) for callers that want them. This differs from my consensus round only in *where* the sync surface lives: previously the handle itself did network IO; now the core never blocks and blocking is a facade, which is the better factoring and the one the repo already uses everywhere else. Note what this does for the REPL round: `yin.repl.index` reads back only what it just wrote, so its publish/readback path (`index.cljc:232-252`) stays entirely on local hits — the round never waits on the network even without any facade.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Unchanged standings:** solo mode is \"no raw port composed\" (D4); `mem` default, `dht:` explicit, the structural `--dht-publish` gate (D9) — with the gate now enforced at **both** ends gpt names: origin (the store refuses to enqueue replication intent for non-public content while closed) and serving (bootstrap-alone is fetch/serve of already-public content, never originating publication). D7 unchanged: local HEAD covers restart; a reader given only a peer address still needs a supplied manifest address until the root-facts epic exists.\n    39\t\n    40\t## R4. Revised slice plan\n    41\t\n    42\tMatching gpt's skeleton (which is the better of the two — gemini's bundles the stream-API rewrite, adapter, and solo mode into one slice and has no contract-freeze step), with my amendments:\n    43\t\n    44\t- **S0 — Contract freeze (docs, no behavior).** Raw value shapes/outcomes, DHT request/outcome values, CBOR wire + `:ver`, chunk fields and all bounds, peers-per-reply cap, cookie/caps design (D6 gate), facade vs staged-hydration boundary; fix the stale `dao.jing.dht.md:19` line and the `dao.jing.cbor.md:469` DHT-envelope sentence; add the raw layer to the design docs. *AC:* every `append!` outcome and every asynchronous completion enumerated; no sentence equates send acceptance with delivery.\n    45\t- **S1 — Raw datagram layer (`dao.stream.datagram`).** Host seams move under it; value channel rebuilt on top; public udp/remote surfaces unchanged. *AC:* per-host and cross-host raw round-trips of exact bytes + observed source; oversize → `invalid-value`; ring gap observable as loss; **all existing `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.stream.remote` tests pass unchanged**.\n    46\t- **S2 — DHT stream core + solo + adapter (parallel with S1 after S0).** Request/outcome streams, caller-stepped interpreter, local verdict semantics, address-only volatile intent, JVM facade, solo net. *AC:* put does no lookup and no peer wait; ids correlate under concurrent requests; solo composes no raw port and no intent queue; file-backed acks survive restart; facade deadlines honored; no threads/callbacks in the core.\n    47\t- **S3 — DHT raw wire + bounded chunk transfer (after S1+S2).** CBOR + `:ver`, rpc correlation, capped peers-per-reply, chunk protocol with shared-utility reassembly, whole-content verification. *AC:* 2–3 node JVM and cross-host transfers of >1200 B and ~64 KiB segments succeed under injected deterministic loss/reordering; oversize and malformed chunks stay within every declared bound; loopback-only exposure.\n    48\t- **S4 — Hardening + lookup repair + storage bound (after S3).** Cookies, source matching, per-source/global caps, `:from`-after-validation, dead-peer shortlist replacement, local storage bound with loud refusal. *AC:* a spoofed source cannot obtain a fragmented reply or force reassembly; over-limit peers cost bounded memory; a live next-nearest peer serves when nearer peers are dead.\n    49\t- **S5 — REPL integration (externally gated on the durable-store epic).** `dht:<dir>` spec, solo default, dir lock (two processes ⇒ two dirs + loopback bootstrap), HEAD unchanged, bootstrap ⇒ fetch/serve, `--dht-publish` gate, remote query from a supplied manifest address with staged hydration. *AC:* local-only restart sequence per the durable findings §5; B's `q` resolves A's manifest over the wire; default start opens no socket.\n    50\t\n    51\t**Concurrency, honestly:** S0 → {S1 ∥ S2} → S3 → S4 → S5, with S1∥S2 legitimate because file ownership is disjoint (raw layer + udp refactor vs `dao/jing/dht*` core) and S0 froze the interfaces both consume. No slice before S5 touches `yin/repl/*`. This matches gpt; gemini's S0∥S1→S2→S3→S4 is the same graph under different names, minus the freeze slice.\n    52\t\n    53\t## R5. Items for the OWNER\n    54\t\n    55\t1. **Publication policy, both ends** (adopt gpt's framing, which sharpens my D9.3): ratify that explicit public opt-in (`--dht-publish`) may publish REPL code before ShiBi, or prohibit all REPL-code publication until ShiBi — and note the gate is enforceable at origin *and* serving, so the answer should say whether serving cached third-party content under bootstrap-alone is acceptable in the interim.\n    56\t2. **Remote-read scope** (gpt's #2, = my standing D7): with local HEAD covering restart, a reader holding only a peer address cannot find the *latest* manifest. Supplied-manifest-address is this epic's scope; automatic latest-root discovery is a separate root-facts epic (signed chain, blocked on naming + ShiBi). The owner should confirm they accept \"supplied address now, root facts later\".\n    57\t3. **The synchronous boundary** (gpt's #3 and gemini's R5 are the same question; my recommendation): ratify the repo pattern — portable non-blocking stream core + JVM-only blocking facade + staged hydration elsewhere — rather than a cascading async refactor of `dao.jing`'s byte-store handle contract, whose blast radius includes `yin.repl.index` and `dao.jing.content`. The cascading refactor is a defensible future epic, not a rider on this one.\n    58\t4. *(Architect-decidable, flagged for transparency, not owner)*: peers-per-reply capped to what fits one 1200 B datagram (nearest ~8, iteration compensates) — it mildly shapes DHT convergence vs traffic, so the owner may care; and the raw layer's name (`dao.stream.datagram`).\n    59\t\n    60\t## Where I agree and differ\n     8\t**D1. Fragmentation home:**\n     9\tMy final position: (c) IDhtNet over a `dao.stream.udp` port / stream channel. (I change my mind).\n    10\tReason: Building fragmentation inside `dao.jing.dht.node` unnecessarily duplicates the chunk sizing and reassembly logic already written and tested in `dao.stream.udp` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151-195, 316-361`). Reusing the stream channel satisfies the owner's stream primacy invariant and isolates the transport layer from Kademlia mechanics.\n    11\t(a) REJECT (duplicates logic). (b) REJECT (ignores existing stream framing). (c) ACCEPT.\n    12\t\n    13\t**D2. DHT wire codec:**\n    14\tMy final position: Switch Transit-JSON -> canonical CBOR now.\n    15\tReason: `docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:464-471` requires the canonical CBOR profile for all Jing boundaries, and `dao.stream.udp` expects CBOR (`udp.cljc:58-62`). Because there is no existing fleet deployed, we can execute a clean break without the complexity of versioned migration negotiation (`dao.jing.cbor.md:496-503`).\n    16\t(a) ACCEPT. (b) REJECT. (c) REJECT.\n    17\t\n    18\t**D3. Replication acknowledgement contract:**\n    19\tMy final position: (a) local durable insert + volatile best-effort replication intent, loss on crash acceptable. (I change my mind from implying a durable queue).\n    20\tReason: `dao.stream.memory-log` is inherently process-lifetime and volatile (`memory_log.cljc:1-6`). Because DHT content replication is idempotent (`docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:83-86`), dropped intent on crash is acceptable and recoverable via a later anti-entropy sweep. The intent lives in the volatile `:outbox` handle.\n    21\t(a) ACCEPT. (b) REJECT (over-engineers a durable queue for re-derivable intent).\n    22\t\n    23\t**D4. Offline / \"local-only\":**\n    24\tMy final position: (b) a solo IDhtNet. (I change my mind).\n    25\tReason: Passing an empty `:bootstrap` to the UDP constructor still binds a socket and starts a receiver daemon thread (`node.cljc:308-332`), which violates the principle of \"network off\". Furthermore, enqueuing to a `memory-log` that never evicts (`memory_log.cljc:7-9`) while offline creates an unbounded memory leak.\n    26\t(a) REJECT. (b) ACCEPT. (c) REJECT.\n    27\t\n    28\t**D5. Non-JVM hosts:**\n    29\tMy final position: (b) IDhtNet over symmetric `dao.stream` channels. (I change my mind).\n    30\tReason: Having accepted D1(c), the transport layer is `dao.stream.udp`. The `dao.stream.remote` design (`docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:386-387`) already anticipates and covers the host-specific primitives (`DatagramSocket`, `dgram`, `RawDatagramSocket`) at the channel layer. This eliminates the need to fork `node.cljc` into three raw socket implementations.\n    31\t(a) REJECT. (b) ACCEPT.\n    32\t\n    33\t**D6. Hardening timing:**\n    34\tMy final position: Designed before/with fragmentation. (I change my mind).\n    35\tReason: Fragmentation drastically increases the amplification vector—from ~12x to ~60x when exploding a single request into multiple 1200-byte fragments. The cookie challenge and token bucket limits must gate slice 1 to prevent severe DDoS abuse.\n    36\t(a) REJECT. (b) ACCEPT.\n    37\t\n    38\t**D7. Root discovery:**\n    39\tMy final position: Deferred outside this epic because the durable HEAD covers the REPL goal.\n    40\tReason: `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:11-15` explicitly dictates the DHT has no roots and routes only content. For this epic's goal (`yin.repl` persistent index), the `<dir>/HEAD` file from the durable-store epic findings fully satisfies local root discovery. Global remote root discovery is blocked on ShiBi and `dao.stream.discovery`.\n    41\t(a) REJECT (violates DHT constraint). (b)/(c) ACCEPT.\n    42\t\n    43\t**D8. Missed defects to fix:**\n    44\tMy final position: Both defects MUST be fixed in this epic.\n    45\tReason: `dht.cljc:214-215` performs synchronous iterative `lookup` on the put path, blocking the REPL even if the queueing is asynchronous. The outbox must queue content addresses, pushing the `lookup` to the runner. Furthermore, dead-peer replacement (`dht.cljc:107`) is required for availability, or partitions will stall fetch operations.\n    46\t(a) ACCEPT.\n    47\t\n    48\t**D9. Owner-policy recommendations:**\n    49\tMy final position: (1) default mem (ACCEPT); (2) network off by default = solo net (ACCEPT); (3) no REPL code on open DHT until ShiBi, structurally enforced (ACCEPT).\n    50\tReason: Documentation alone cannot prevent accidental data exfiltration. Since publication is irreversible (`docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:13`), initiating network replication of locally evaluated ASTs must require an explicit flag (e.g., `--dht-publish`) beyond merely providing a bootstrap peer.\n    51\t\n    52\t**D10. Final slice plan:**\n    53\tMy final position:\n    54\t1. **Contract & Local-only**: Implement solo `IDhtNet`, async volatile outbox mechanism enqueuing addresses (fixing synchronous lookup), dead-peer shortlist replacement, and document the volatile intent contract.\n    55\t2. **Hardening Design & Transport**: Design cookie/rate-limits, then implement `IDhtNet` over a `dao.stream.udp` port (`udp.cljc:202-261`), transitioning wire to CBOR.\n    56\t3. **Hardening Implementation**: Implement cookie challenges for fragmenting replies and LRU-capped per-source rate limits.\n    57\t4. **Host Seams**: Implement `dao.stream` UDP channels for Node/CLJD.\n    58\t5. **yin.repl Integration**: Wire the REPL with the `--dht-publish` structural gate (externally dependent on the durable-store epic for the `dht:<dir>` spec).\n    59\t*Concurrency*: Slice 1 is independent. Slices 2, 3, and 4 modify the transport/receiver sequential path and should not run concurrently to avoid merge conflicts. Slice 5 waits on the durable-store epic.\n    60\t(a) ACCEPT.\n    61\t\n    62\t**Resolved vs Escalated:**\n    63\tI consider disputes **D1 through D10 fully RESOLVED** by this consensus round. The team has reached agreement on the safest architectural boundaries (GLM's transport and offline rulings). No disputes require escalation to the OWNER.\n    64\t\n     8\tConsensus round on the dao.jing.dht epic. Positions weighed: author r1+r2 (gemini-3.1-pro-high), gpt-6-sol critique, and my own round-1 critique. Where I change my round-1 position, I say so explicitly.\n     9\t\n    10\t**D1. Fragmentation home.** FINAL: **(c) — `IDhtNet` over a `dao.stream.udp` port** (unchanged from round 1). `fragment-envelope` is private (`udp.cljc:151-157`) and the machinery that makes it safe — chunk sizing (`udp.cljc:168-195`), oversize refusal (`udp.cljc:225-233`), bounded reassembly with eviction (`udp.cljc:316-361`), source-address-only replies (`udp.cljc:373-399`) — is reusable without duplication only if the DHT rides the channel; a node-local copy (author r2 slice 1 still specifies exactly that) is a second security-sensitive framing implementation in one tree, and gpt's (b) run as an *extraction* is the acceptable fallback, not (a) as written. (b) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: acceptable only as one shared fragmenter extracted to a common namespace that *both* `dao.stream.udp` and the DHT require — not a copy — with DHT message identity aligned to the envelope's id semantics and an explicit request/answer marker replacing `direction-of`'s `:dao.stream.remote/op` test (`udp.cljc:143-148`); it costs three per-host socket stacks that (c) avoids. (a) **REJECT** as specified (copying a private envelope shape into `node.cljc` duplicates the one thing the round agrees must not be duplicated).\n    11\t\n    12\t**D2. Wire codec.** FINAL: **CBOR now, conditionally on D1=(c)** — a refinement of my round-1 \"decide/freeze before a fleet exists\": the decision is downstream of the transport fork, and the fork resolves to (c). If the DHT rides the §3.2 channel, \"One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile\" (`dao.stream.remote.md:363-366`) makes CBOR not a choice but the channel's rule, and keeping Transit would mean carrying Transit text inside CBOR fragment envelopes. The clean break without version negotiation (`dao.jing.cbor.md:496-503`) is free only while no fleet exists, so it must ride slice 1. Author's switch-now **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION** (scope: wire only — `IDhtNet` at `dht.cljc:53-86` and the store handle unchanged; Base64-in-application-value stays per `dao.jing.cbor.md:464-471`; `dao.jing.cbor.md:469`'s DHT-envelope sentence updated with it). gpt's keep-the-wire **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: only under D1=(b)/(a), where the DHT owns its framing and `dao.stream.transit` is genuinely portable (`transit.cljc:6-10` has clj/cljs/cljd branches); under (c) it contradicts the channel rule.\n    13\t\n    14\t**D3. Replication acknowledgement contract.** FINAL: **(a)** (unchanged). The author's r2 converged here in substance — \"The replication queue is ephemeral and lost on process crash\" — which resolves the round-1 contradiction; what remains is to make the sentence exact and pin the local backend. Reasons: `memory-log` is process-lifetime by its own contract (`memory_log.cljc:3-6`), replication is idempotent and convergent (`dao.jing.dht.md:83-86`) so intent is re-derivable from the local store, which is therefore the *only* durable record needed; a durable intent log (b) duplicates that record, adds an fsync-class cost per put to the path this epic exists to unblock (`dht.cljc:216-223`), and creates a second thing to reconcile after crashes. Where intent lives: a volatile `memory-log` of **content addresses** in the handle (addresses, not peer targets — the put path's synchronous `lookup`, `dht.cljc:214-215`, must move to the runner too; see D8); the durable basis is `:local`, and \"durably stored locally\" may only be claimed when `:local` is `dao.jing.file` — the generic constructor today accepts mem (`dht.cljc:285-311`), so the `dht:` spec must pin file and the docstring must condition the word. (b) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: coherent only if the owner explicitly wants \"acked ⇒ held on ≥1 remote peer,\" and then as an explicit file-backed intent stream recorded before ack, with defined retry/recovery/shutdown — I would not build it for this epic; **REJECT** as the default.\n    15\t\n    16\t**D4. Offline / local-only.** FINAL: **(b) solo `IDhtNet`** (unchanged) — and I note this is now effectively 2-of-3: gpt's policy B2 independently defines local-only as \"no listener, outbound dial, or replication worker,\" citing the same evidence I did — `create-node` binds a socket and starts a daemon receiver unconditionally (`node.cljc:308-332`, `start-receiver!` at `node.cljc:144-200`). The author's r2 (a) still ships the socket+thread and patches policy on top (\"runner drops the replication request if there are no known peers\"), which is nominal OFF, and drops-per-request is wasted work for a state that should not exist. Solo is a one-screen value (`known-peers []`, `lookup []`), makes \"no socket opens\" assertable in an acceptance test, and makes \"never enqueue while offline\" structural rather than a runner courtesy — the alternative queue-and-grow is unbounded because a memory-log never evicts (`memory_log.cljc:7-9`). (a) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: only as a transitional shim, and only if the default path provably binds nothing. (c) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: coherent only under D3=(b); under (a) there is nothing to retain — offline ⇒ never enqueue, and a later restart sweep or explicit re-publish covers peers appearing late. **REJECT** under D3=(a).\n    17\t\n    18\t**D5. Non-JVM hosts.** FINAL: **(b)** (unchanged). This is D1 seen from the host axis: under (c), non-JVM support is two thin seams feeding `receive!` for exactly the primitives `dao.stream.remote.md:386-388` names (Node `dgram`, cljd `RawDatagramSocket`; browsers have no UDP), the browser path becomes a channel swap (ws §3.1 / pair relay §3.3), and — decisive with D6 — hardening is written **once** at the stream layer instead of three times. The author's (a) triple-versions the wire, codec, reassembly bounds, and rate limiting across hosts, which is where security defects concentrate. (a) **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: as a strict interim only — same wire/codec/fragmenter/hardening core shared, per-host code limited to the socket calls, superseded by (b) when the channel lands; **REJECT** as three independent peers as specified in the author's r2 slice 3.\n    19\t\n    20\t**D6. Hardening timing.** FINAL: **design gates slice 1; implementation lands before any non-loopback exposure and before host spread** — I accept the author's r2 slice-4 *ordering* for the implementation while keeping my round-1 design-first gate, and gpt's A4 (\"an architectural threat-model decision followed by implementation work\") shows all three now agree on design-first; the residual is only whether it gets written down at slice 1. Evidence for the gate: fragmentation turns today's ~12× worst case (~1200 B reply to a ~100 B request, `node.cljc:76-80` and `node.cljc:190-195`) into ~60× (64 KiB across ~60 datagrams, `udp.cljc:65-68`), so the amplification budget, cookie scheme, and caps must be named in slice 1's contract even if coded in slice 4. The author's r2 cookie is now concrete and mostly right (stateless HMAC, LRU cache); adopt it with gpt's additions: bind the HMAC to observed source **ip:port** + a time bucket (not IP alone), and match replies to the expected source, not merely the rpc number (`node.cljc:176-178` delivers by `:rpc` only) — a per-`claimed-:from` bucket is spoofable, as gpt says. Author's late-slice **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION** (conditions: slice-1 written threat model; loopback-only until landed). My round-1 stance stands; no change of mind, only convergence in wording.\n    21\t\n    22\t**D7. Root discovery.** FINAL: **out of this epic** (unchanged) — and the author converged (r2's slice list contains no root slice; their r1 already placed it \"strictly OUTSIDE the DHT\"). The epic's goal — `dht:` as *this process's* persistent store — is fully served by `<dir>/HEAD` (durable findings §2, line 16); root facts need writer authentication that does not exist (peer names are unauthenticated until ShiBi, `dao.stream.remote.md:503-506`), a naming/rendezvous layer that is proposal-only (`dao.stream.discovery.md`: \"Nothing here is implemented\"), and a signed monotonic chain is precisely a root of authority the DHT is forbidden to be (`dao.jing.dht.md:11-15`). gpt's signed-chain design (`{writer-id, sequence, previous-root, manifest-address}` over a configured stream, verified key + monotonicity) is the right seed — carry it verbatim into the separate epic's notes. gpt's in-epic slice **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION**: only if the owner explicitly widens this epic's goal to cross-process sharing *now*, and then without any rendezvous dependency (configured descriptors only); **REJECT** inside the REPL-store epic.\n    23\t\n    24\t**D8. Missed defects — in or out?** FINAL: **all three in, split across slices.** (1) Synchronous `lookup` on the put path (`dht.cljc:214-215`, each hop 500 ms × 3 tries, `node.cljc:236-241`) — **in, slice 2**: the outbox carries content addresses and the runner owns peer resolution; without this the outbox only removes half the blocking. (2) Lookup stranding live peers behind dead closest-peers (`dht.cljc:107-131`; named open at `dao.jing.dht.md:180-184`) — **in, slice 4**: it is the read path this epic's foreign-fetch goal exercises, and under partition each missing address can cost k × 1.5 s; prune-and-expand to k *responsive* peers. (3) Storage limits / availability repair — **split**: in-epic is the *local* bound knob with explicit refuse/degrade when hit (a node that accepts content it cannot hold is lying; today caching is unbounded, `dao.jing.dht.md:172-174`), plus an optional bounded \"republish local content on join\" (which is also D3(a)'s restart sweep); **out** of this epic: the full repair/republish subsystem and GC/pinning — same deferral class as round 1, and the single-owner availability story is HEAD + the local file store. gpt's fuller availability scope **ACCEPT-WITH-CONDITION** (the bounded republish option), **REJECT** as an in-epic subsystem.\n    25\t\n    26\t**D9. Owner-policy recommendations.**\n    27\t(1) **Default `mem`, `dht:` explicit — all three agree; RESOLVED.** Ratify as written, with gpt's addition that a `dht:` choice names a durable directory (it does: `dht:<dir>`), and my addition to document the three postures (mem / `file:<dir>` / `dht:<dir>`).\n    28\t(2) **Network off by default = solo net — 2-of-3 with the author's substance converging; RESOLVED** (it is D4; the residual is implementation shape, not policy). gpt's \"bootstrap addresses are ordinary peers, never authorities\" and my \"announce the bound host:port when a socket opens\" are compatible additions; adopt both.\n    29\t(3) **Structurally enforced, not documented — FINAL: structural** (unchanged; gpt's B3 independently reaches the same mechanism: \"A warning alone does not enforce it\"). I adopt gpt's broader definition of sensitive — *anything the REPL can publish that the user has not explicitly marked public* (source text, literals/secrets, AST rows, execution state, metadata, index nodes) — over my narrower \"everything the indexer publishes,\" because the broader phrasing extends correctly to future intake kinds even though the sets coincide today (`yin/repl/index.cljc:111-115` publishes code+provenance). Mechanism: `dht:` with bootstrap is fetch/cache/serve-only; originating publication of locally indexed content requires a second explicit opt-in (e.g. `--dht-publish`), with a clear statement that peers can retain and redistribute every segment — irreversibility is the reason this must be a gate, not advice: no deletes by design (`dao.jing.dht.md:13`), replication to k strangers, integrity never confidentiality (`dao.stream.remote.md` §1, §7). The author's r2 stance is documentation-only: **REJECT** as sufficient; it is fine as the *wording* that accompanies the gate.\n    30\t\n    31\t**D10. Final slice plan.** FINAL (my round-1 plan, amended by this round — changes: gpt's contract-first step folded in as S0; shortlist pruning and the storage-bound knob added to S4; the D1/D5 decisions assumed):\n    32\t\n    33\t- **S0 — Contract & docs (no behavior).** Write the decided contracts: ack sentence (D3a: ack = local verdict; volatile intent), local-only = solo net (D4), threat model + cookie + caps (D6 design), exposure posture (loopback-only until S4), codec freeze (D2). Fix the stale `dao.jing.dht.md:19` sha256-only line and `dao.jing.cbor.md:469`. *AC:* docs merged; no code.\n    34\t- **S1 — Transport + CBOR + fragmentation (JVM).** `IDhtNet` over a `dao.stream.udp` port (`make-port` + `send-to!`, `udp.cljc:202-261`), `DatagramSocket` seam, one CBOR value per reassembled datagram; `IDhtNet` signature frozen here. *AC:* 2- and 3-node JVM put/get of segments > 1200 B and > 64 KiB; oversize refused `:dao.stream.remote/oversize`, never torn; replies to the datagram source only; existing `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc` + `dht/node_test.cljc` green.\n    35\t- **S2 — Async replication outbox (parallel with S3 after S1).** Outbox of content addresses; runner owns lookup + `store-content!`; caller-stepped or injectable schedule (`dao.jing.md:601-608` precedent); stopped by `close-fn`. *AC:* put returns bounded (< ~50 ms) with all peers blackholed; late peers eventually receive; close leaves no pump/thread; crash loses only intent, never content.\n    36\t- **S3 — Host seams: Node `dgram`, CLJD `RawDatagramSocket` (parallel with S2; after S1).** *AC:* clj↔node and clj↔cljd multi-fragment put/get proofs, mirroring the existing cross-host socket proofs.\n    37\t- **S4 — Hardening + pruning + storage bound (after S1; before any non-loopback exposure).** Cookie (HMAC over observed ip:port + time bucket, LRU), LRU-capped per-source token buckets, per-source reassembly cap, reply-source matching, dead-peer replacement, local storage bound with explicit refusal. *AC:* unverified source wanting a fragmenting reply gets a cookie, never datagrams; over-limit peer refused with bounded memory; dead shortlist member replaced by next-nearest reachable in bounded rounds; store over bound refuses loudly.\n    38\t- **S5 — `yin.repl` integration — externally gated on the durable-store epic** (its spec/HEAD/lock slices; the mechanism does not exist yet — no `--index-store` in `main.cljc`). `dht:<dir>` spec; solo net when no peers; dir lock via the local file store, two processes ⇒ two dirs + loopback bootstrap; HEAD unchanged; bootstrap ⇒ fetch/serve; `--dht-publish` gate. *AC:* local-only restart sequence per durable findings §5; B's `q` resolves a manifest A published; default start opens no socket (assertable).\n    39\t- **Deferred (separate epics):** root-facts stream (seed = gpt's signed chain; blocked on discovery + ShiBi), browser/ws-channel `IDhtNet`, GC/pinning/full repair, NAT traversal (`dao.stream.remote.md` §4).\n    40\t\n    41\tConcurrency, honestly: S0 → S1 → {S2 ∥ S3} → S4 → S5, with S2∥S3 permitted only because S1 freezes `IDhtNet` — the author's r2 now sequences 1→3→4 on `node.cljc` and holds S2 parallel, which matches this once the interface freeze is explicit; none of S0–S4 touches `yin/repl/*`.\n    42\t\n    43\t---\n    44\t\n    45\t**RESOLVED by this round** (my assessment of mob consensus, not just my agreement):\n    46\t1. **D3** — the round-1 contradiction is gone: all three now say ephemeral queue, loss-on-crash acceptable (author r2 adopted it explicitly). Residual is wording + pinning `:local` to file, not substance.\n    47\t2. **D4 (policy half)** — local-only means no listener/worker (glm + gpt; author's drop-when-peerless converges in substance). Residual is solo-net vs empty-bootstrap as implementation, which follows D1/D5.\n    48\t3. **D6 (design-first)** — all three agree the threat model precedes fragmentation; only the write-it-down point remained, and the author's r2 cookie design supplies the content.\n    49\t4. **D7** — root discovery is outside this epic; HEAD is this epic's root mechanism. The author dropped it from r2's slices; gpt's signed chain is banked as the separate epic's seed.\n    50\t5. **D8 (in-epic part)** — sync-lookup fix and dead-peer pruning are in this epic (slices 2 and 4); nobody dissents.\n    51\t6. **D9.1 and D9.2** — mem default; network-off-by-default. Unanimous.\n    52\t7. **D10 skeleton** — transport/contract first, replication second, hardening before exposure, REPL last and gated on the durable-store epic; sequencing honesty restored (author r2 corrected round 1's \"1–4 concurrent\").\n    53\t\n    54\t**Must go to the OWNER:**\n    55\t1. **D1/D5 — the transport fork.** The one genuine architectural decision: `IDhtNet` over the `dao.stream.udp` channel (my recommendation, 2-of-3 leaning) versus a standalone versioned DHT wire. It commits the DHT to the stream layer, shapes slices 1/3/4, and is a statement about how far stream primacy reaches; the owner should ratify, not inherit, it.\n    56\t2. **D2 — the codec clean break.** Follows from D1, but it is irreversible-in-practice before any fleet exists (`dao.jing.cbor.md:496-503`: no version negotiation) and deserves an explicit owner yes.\n    57\t3. **D9.3 — ratify the structural publish gate** (`--dht-publish` or equivalent) and the definition of sensitive. All three favor restriction; two of three favor structural enforcement; it is an owner policy, and the cost of the gate (an extra step before sharing) is the owner's to accept.\n    58\t4. **D3 — only if the owner wants \"acked ⇒ on ≥1 remote peer.\"** Default to (a) unless they say otherwise; (b) is real scope the epic does not otherwise need.\n    59\t5. **D8 — the local storage-bound default** (a resource policy: refuse vs degrade when a peer's store is full).\n    60\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-30 09:40:47 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 16:40:47 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n## 1. Raw datagram layer — R1\n\n**Synthesis for ratification: `dao.stream.datagram`.** This adopts Gemini’s and GLM’s namespace over my earlier `dao.stream.udp.raw`: a datagram is the lower abstraction, while `dao.stream.udp` remains the existing decoded-value channel. The owner explicitly requires a raw datagram layer exposed as `dao.stream` and a DHT built on it ([revision brief](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob-revision.prompt.md:15)). This **reverses my previous D1 recommendation** that the DHT ride the value/fragment channel.\n\nA bound port has a `dao.stream` writer handle and a caller-supplied inbound traffic stream. The writer accepts:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream.datagram/destination {:host h :port p}\n :dao.stream.datagram/bytes       b64}\n```\n\nThe host adapter appends received events to the traffic stream:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream.datagram/source {:host observed-h :port observed-p}\n :dao.stream.datagram/bytes  b64}\n```\n\n`b64` is padded standard Base64 of the **exact datagram bytes**. I pick my and GLM’s position over Gemini’s host byte arrays: byte arrays have different host types, while the existing remote-content convention already uses Base64 in stream-visible values ([content.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:11)). Encoding here is representation of raw bytes in a portable stream value; the raw layer does not decode a DHT or CBOR message.\n\nThe writer’s `descriptor` is a plain-data local-port descriptor, for example `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/datagram, :dao.stream/identity id, :dao.stream.datagram/host h, :dao.stream.datagram/port p}`. Binding creates the port; a destination is supplied **per append**, so no per-destination writer is required. A descriptor does not promise a reader on the socket. The socket retains no datagrams and has no reader surface: readers use independent cursors on the explicitly supplied traffic stream, whose retention is chosen at composition. A bounded ring’s `:dao.stream/gap` means missed datagram events and is handled by the consumer’s timeout/retry policy. This follows the stream contract’s boundary rule ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:445)).\n\nSet the admitted decoded datagram limit to 1200 bytes, matching the current UDP budget ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:58)). Malformed destination or Base64 and oversize sends yield `:dao.stream/invalid-value` with no send. A closed port yields `:dao.stream/closed`; an actual host send fault yields `:dao.stream/transport-error`; `:dao.stream/ok` means local acceptance, not remote receipt. If a composed bounded outbound queue refuses a write, its outcome is `:dao.stream/full`. **Gemini’s suggested writer `:dao.stream/blocked` is rejected:** `blocked` is a reader outcome, while `full` is the writer outcome ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:571), [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:588)). An oversized inbound datagram is invalid transport input and is dropped with a bounded diagnostic before deposit.\n\nMove the existing JVM, Node, and Dart socket seams under this port. They already expose raw received bytes, observed source, addressed send, and close ([jvm.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj:12), [node.cljs](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs:14), [dart.cljd](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd:13)). Their host callbacks only deposit and return. Rebuild `dao.stream.udp` as an interpreter reading raw traffic and writing raw datagrams; preserve its public descriptor, `send-to!`, fragmentation, projection, and `step!` behavior. `dao.stream.remote` keeps using that value channel unchanged. Today `dao.stream.udp` takes a direct host `:send!` and receives bytes directly ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:202), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:373)); those become internal adapters over the raw writer and traffic stream.\n\n## 2. DHT on raw datagrams — R2\n\n**Pick canonical CBOR with a wire-version field, for ratification.** This changes my revision-round recommendation to retain Transit-JSON. GLM supplied a concrete reason independent of the value channel: the current `find-node` response can contain up to `k=20` peer maps ([kad.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/kad.cljc:9), [node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:101)); an oversized response is presently dropped without a reply ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:186)). CBOR reduces envelope cost, but **codec alone is not a size proof**. Cap peers per reply at a number validated against encoded size, initially at most eight, and let iterative lookup request further peers. Every DHT wire message carries `:ver 1`; unknown versions receive no large reply. This is a clean wire break before a deployed fleet, and the current Transit envelope description must be updated ([dao.jing.cbor.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:462)).\n\nThe DHT consumes the **raw** traffic stream and emits addressed raw datagrams. It owns RPC IDs, expected-source matching, deadlines, retries, and its Kademlia interpretation. A small encoded RPC is one datagram. Large RPC values or segment transfers use **DHT-owned** chunk records containing version, RPC ID, request/reply direction, part index, total parts, and bytes. Reassembly is keyed by observed source address and port, direction, and RPC ID. The DHT must not import `dao.stream.udp`’s private `:dao.stream.remote/id` fragment envelope ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151)). A shared transport-neutral chunk/reassembly utility is acceptable if both layers actually use it; their wire fields and identities remain separate.\n\nFreeze an encoded-message maximum and per-source/global limits in S0. I favor the existing value layer’s 64 KiB default as an initial **bound**, subject to measuring real index nodes before freezing it; a node larger than the chosen limit must fail explicitly, never become a silently local-only segment. Check total encoded size before any chunk is sent; ignore duplicates, reject inconsistent counts, cap accumulated bytes and partial-message count, and evict incomplete assemblies. Loss causes an entire-RPC retry. After reassembly, validate canonical content bytes and the requested content hash before storage or cache insertion ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:167)).\n\nHardening is implemented in the DHT interpreter **where it reads raw events**, not as semantics of the general raw port. A small challenge obtains a time-limited cookie bound to the observed source IP and port. Require proof before fragmented replies or significant incoming reassembly; validate a token on incoming chunks before allocating partial state. Bound unverified replies, per-source and global work, partials, pending RPCs, and stored content. Match replies against both RPC ID and expected observed source; observe claimed `:from` routing data only after validation. The current node observes `:from` before processing and delivers replies by RPC ID alone ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:171)). Specify these protections with the chunk protocol; non-loopback exposure waits for their implementation.\n\n## 3. DHT stream API and Jing adapter — R3\n\nCompose a **request stream**, **terminal-outcome stream**, and separate **replication-progress stream**. Each request has a caller-minted ID:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :put\n :dao.jing.dht/address address :dao.jing.dht/bytes b64}\n{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :get\n :dao.jing.dht/address address}\n{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :find\n :dao.jing.dht/target target-id}\n```\n\nA request writer’s `append!` reports only admission to that stream. A caller-driven `step!` reads requests and raw traffic, advances explicit cursors and pending-RPC state, and appends one terminal value per admitted request ID. Put outcomes carry the local `:inserted` or `:present` verdict; get outcomes carry verified found bytes, not-found, timeout, refusal, or error; find outcomes carry bounded peer data. These DHT-qualified values are **not new `dao.stream` operation outcomes**. The stream operation vocabulary remains exhaustive ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:96)). IDs, rather than addresses alone, distinguish concurrent and repeated requests; define duplicate-ID refusal and bounded completion retention.\n\nThe standing D3 contract is unchanged: an acknowledgement is the **local backend verdict**, durable only if that backend is durable. Replication intent is a volatile address-only log; the stepped runner performs lookup and sends after the local verdict. It may be lost on crash, and a later explicit reconciliation sweep can derive work from retained content. Current `make-put` performs both lookup and peer waits before returning ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:203)). Solo mode composes no raw port and enqueues no replication work; empty bootstrap on the current UDP constructor still opens a socket ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:307)).\n\n**Jing compatibility needs two explicit paths.** The portable store handle’s `put-bytes-fn` validates and writes the local backend, emits replication intent, and returns `:inserted|:present`; its `get-bytes-fn` answers local content immediately. This preserves the synchronous publication/readback round in `yin.repl.index`, which reads the manifest it just wrote ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:232)), and it remains a valid byte-store handle for `dao.jing.content` ([content.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:39)). For a **remote miss**, use a separate stream request and staged hydration, then retry the local read. An optional JVM blocking facade may drive steps to a deadline and preserve current synchronous remote-get behavior; it is not the portable core. This follows the repository’s stepped-client/blocking-driver split ([content.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:18)). **Gemini’s spin-polling store adapter is rejected:** a byte-store `get` cannot return an “outstanding” marker as if it were content or not-found, and a portable stream operation cannot wait for a remote answer ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:80)). Document the compatibility change from today’s synchronously fetching DHT `get` ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:227)).\n\n## 4. Revised slice plan — R4\n\n| Slice | Files or areas | Acceptance and dependency |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S0 — contract freeze** | `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md`, `dao.jing.dht.md`, `dao.jing.cbor.md`; new raw-layer design section | Specify raw descriptor/values/outcomes, DHT stream values, CBOR v1 wire, measured size cap, chunk keys and limits, cookie gate, and local/remote Jing semantics. Correct the stale SHA-256-only claim: the implementation accepts registered algorithms ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:17), [dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:299)). |\n| **S1 — raw datagram stream** | New `src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc`; existing JVM/Node/Dart UDP host seams; `src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc` | Exact byte/source round trips on all hosts; oversize gives `invalid-value`; bounded-traffic gap is observable; all existing `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.stream.remote` behavior tests pass unchanged. |\n| **S2 — DHT stream core and solo mode** | `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc`; new DHT interpreter/adapter files; separate tests | Concurrent request IDs correlate; put performs no network lookup or peer wait; file-backed local verdict survives restart; solo mode opens no port or volatile queue; optional JVM facade respects deadlines. |\n| **S3 — DHT raw wire and chunks** | `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`, DHT wire/chunk module and tests | Depends on S1 and S2. Two or three peers exchange small RPCs and multi-datagram segments under loss and reordering; oversized values refuse before send; malformed fragments stay within limits; only loopback exposure. |\n| **S4 — hardening, lookup, storage bound** | DHT wire/receiver, `dht.cljc`, `dht/kad.cljc` as needed and tests | Spoofed traffic cannot trigger fragmented amplification or substantial reassembly; source-mismatched replies are ignored; a live next-nearest peer is tried after dead peers; full local storage refuses explicitly. Non-loopback exposure is gated here. |\n| **S5 — REPL integration** | `yin/repl/*` and REPL tests | Depends on S4 **and** the separate durable-store epic’s spec, HEAD, lock, and recovery work ([durable-store findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:7)). Default `mem`; explicit `dht:<dir>` with solo mode and no socket; two processes use separate locked dirs; supplied-manifest remote hydration supports query; publication/serving gate is enforced. |\n\nS1 and S2 may run concurrently **after S0**, with disjoint source and test ownership. S3 joins them; S4 follows S3 because it changes the same DHT receive path. S5 waits for both epics. **No S0–S4 implementation touches `yin/repl/*`.** Full pinning, GC, network availability repair, NAT meeting, browser transport, and automatic latest-root discovery remain separate work; bounded refusal and dead-peer replacement are in this epic.\n\n## 5. Differences and picks for ratifiers\n\n| Issue | Positions | Synthesis pick and reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw namespace | Gemini/GLM: `dao.stream.datagram`; GPT: `dao.stream.udp.raw` | **Gemini/GLM.** The lower layer should not be named as a child of the higher UDP value channel. |\n| Stream-visible bytes | Gemini: host byte arrays; GPT/GLM: Base64 | **GPT/GLM.** Base64 is portable plain data and matches the existing remote-content value convention. |\n| Raw writer backpressure | Gemini: `blocked`; GPT/GLM: writer outcome set | **GPT/GLM.** `blocked` is a read outcome; `full` is the write outcome. |\n| DHT codec | Gemini/GLM: CBOR; GPT revision: keep Transit initially | **Gemini/GLM, with GPT’s explicit version field.** Full `find-node` replies already risk the datagram budget; CBOR plus an encoded-size-tested peer cap is a better v1 wire. This is the principal ratification point. |\n| Large-message handling | All: DHT-owned wire chunks, not `dao.stream.udp` envelope; GPT/GLM allow shared utility | **Shared utility only if actually used by both; DHT-owned wire fields.** It preserves the owner’s raw-layer boundary. |\n| Jing adapter | Gemini: synchronous spin-poll/blocked bridge; GPT/GLM: local byte-store plus staged hydration, optional JVM blocking facade | **GPT/GLM.** It preserves the byte-store’s exact result contract without blocking portable stream operations. |\n| Slicing | Gemini: starts extraction immediately; GPT/GLM: contract freeze first | **GPT/GLM.** The new raw descriptor, wire break, chunk security gate, and sync/async seam must be fixed before separate file lanes run. |\n\nThe owner’s direction supersedes previous D1 and changes D5’s mechanism. D3 (local-verdict acknowledgement), D4 (solo mode), D7 (roots outside DHT), D8 (lookup repair and resource bounds), and D9 (memory default, explicit network and disclosure) remain substantively intact.\n\n## 6. Owner decisions — R5\n\n1. **Publication:** May a separate, explicit public-index opt-in publish REPL code before ShiBi, or is all REPL-code publication prohibited until ShiBi? The decision must govern both originating replication and serving locally held content. “Bootstrap configured” alone is not a public-data declaration.\n2. **Acknowledgement:** Ratify the standing local-verdict, volatile-intent contract. If the owner instead needs “acknowledged means remotely replicated,” that is a stronger durable-intent and remote-ack epic.\n3. **Roots:** Ratify local HEAD for restart and a **supplied manifest address** for this epic’s remote query. A reader holding only a peer address cannot discover the latest root; signed root facts and rendezvous require a separate design ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:9), [durable-store findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:16)).\n4. **Compatibility:** Ratify the portable stepped DHT plus local Jing byte-store adapter and optional JVM blocking facade. Portable remote misses use staged hydration. This is a design choice because it changes today’s synchronous DHT miss behavior."}}
